new file mode 100644
index 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000..e9fc157d85b589a3e2d4b2aa418332ce7b55dc33
--- /dev/null
+++ b/docs/virtual-amiibo.md
@@ -0,0 +1,16 @@
+# Virtual amiibo on macOS (1.4.3)
+
+The macOS menu **游戏 → 扫描虚拟 amiibo…** provides an offline Zelda model list and imports a local tag file. Enable amiibo in the game's own options, select the amiibo ability, choose a valid spot, then scan. A scan queued before the game starts searching is delivered at the next detection. Canceling the picker does not queue a new tag.
+
+The host implements one virtual NFP reader. It supplies model information and a stable seven-byte UID, signals the game's detection events, and leaves reward selection and daily limits to the game. It does not read physical NFC hardware or edit game rewards, inventories or saves. Selecting the same catalog model uses the same UID across launches; there is no automatic UID randomization.
+
+The built-in model IDs and names are factual metadata from [AmiiboAPI's database](https://github.com/N3evin/AmiiboAPI/blob/master/database/amiibo.json), snapshot 2026-10-21. No images, cryptographic keys or tag-secret data are bundled. The NFP field layouts and device states were checked against [libnx nfc.h](https://github.com/switchbrew/libnx/blob/master/nx/include/switch/services/nfc.h). Code is implemented locally rather than importing an emulator's implementation.
+
+Supported local imports:
+
+- JSON: `{"amiiboId":"0100000004180902","uid":"04010000000418"}`. The model ID is exactly 16 hexadecimal characters; the optional UID is exactly 14. Omitting it derives a stable UID from the model ID. Arbitrary emulator JSON schemas are not accepted.
+- BIN: standard 532-, 540- or 572-byte tag files. Only the public model bytes at 0x54 and UID bytes are read. The importer does not decrypt or rewrite the file.
+
+`runtime/nfp_virtual.h` owns the bounded state machine; `amiibo_picker.inc` owns the macOS picker. Diagnostic input accepts `amiibo <model-id> <uid>` through the isolated test command file. This queues a scan through the same state machine as the picker.
+
+`tests/nfp_test.c` exercises lifecycle transitions, input rejection, event delivery, repeated scans, model byte order and buffer canaries. `scripts/probes/thread_probe.c` additionally exercises 600 service-event close/rebind cycles in the real SVC dispatcher. These host tests do not establish game acceptance; see the outer project's dated acceptance record and candidate report for the exact binary tested.
